# 🔴 THE LINE ENDINGS OF THESE SCRIPTS ARE PART OF WHAT THE TOOL PRODUCES, so
# they are pinned rather than left to whatever a checkout decides.
#
# lib/_common.ps1 holds the boot script as a here-string, and a here-string
# carries its FILE's line endings into every conversation that is opened. This
# repo is committed with LF and `core.autocrlf` is true, which means the blobs
# are LF and a FRESH CLONE would check them out as CRLF - so the same source
# would write a different boot script on another machine, and the byte-for-byte
# comparison in `rebuild` (plan item 2.5c, `launch/boot-script`) would go red on
# a machine where nothing was wrong.
#
# 🪤 This is not cosmetic and it is not about diffs. The operator works across
# machines, and "it passes here" was the only thing holding that case up.
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.psm1 text eol=lf

# The same reasoning for the C# that mirrors it: `BootScript` pins its own
# newlines with an explicit join, but nothing else should drift underneath the
# tests that read these files.
*.cs text eol=lf
